8/12/99
emc


The icon mapper script is used to create a complete .sue file for the 
specified icon(s).  There are 3 types of .sue files which can be created:

(1) MMI icons only

(2) MMI icons mapped to the user's icons

(3) MMI icons with real schematics


The Three Modes
===============
The following is a full description of the three modes:

(1) MMI icons only

    In this mode, the .sue file created by the mapper contains only an ICON_
    proc, there is no SCHEMATIC_ proc.  These .sue files can be used for drawing,
    but not timing simulation or placement.

    This mode allows the user to draw "generic" schematics using the MMI icons,
    which can be mapped to a schematic library at a future date, as described
    for mode (2) and (3).

    Invocation:  mapper.tcl -icononly ...

(2) MMI icons mapped to the user's icons

    This mode is useful when you wish to draw a schematic using the MMI icons
    but in truth you are designing with another pre-existing library.  This
    mode is primarily useful in allowing the designer to draw "generic"
    schematics using the MMI icons, which can then be mapped to any available
    library, even re-mapped to another library, without requiring changes to
    the schematics.

    In this mode, the .sue file created by the mapper script contains an ICON_
    proc as for option (1), and a SCHEMATIC_ proc which consists of a placement
    of the user's icon, with pins correctly named to match the MMI icon.

    Invocation:  mapper.tcl ...

    Note that the <library-directory>'s contain the user's icon+schematic .sue files

(3) MMI icons with real schematics

    This mode is used to create normal .sue files which contain an ICON_ proc
    as well as a SCHEMATIC_ proc.  The user must first draw the complete set of
    sue schematics for the desired library.  Then the mapper script is run to
    create the final complete .sue files, which include the user's schematic
    and the MMI icons.

    As in mode (2), this method allows a user to draw "generic" schematics
    and potentially re-map the MMI icons to another library at a future date.
    However this method avoids the level of indirection found in mode (2),
    and eliminates the need for a user to create their own library's icons.

    Note that the pin names in the user's schematics must match the pin names
    in the MMI icons.

    Invocation:  mapper.tcl -realschematics -verilog ...

    Note that the <library-directory>'s contain the schematic .sue files.


Mapper Script Invocation
========================
The mapper is invoked as follows:

mapper.tcl <mapfile> <to-directory> <mmi-body-directory> \
   <library-directory> <library-directory> ...

For example, the TSMC25 files were created using this command:

mapper.tcl maptsmc25 tsmc25 mmi_body /proj/tech/tsmc25/stdcell/sue \
   /proj/tech/tsmc25/stdcell/demorgan

Note in this example, the schematic files were found in multiple library-directories.


The mapper script takes the following optional switches:

-icononly:        Generates .sue files that contain only ICON procs.
-verilog:         Includes verilog property in ICON proc.  This should 
		  only be used if the SCHEMATIC proc contains a real
                  schematic; it should not be used if the
                  SCHEMATIC proc contains the placement of a user's icon.
-date:            Allows the user to specify a date to be used in the 
		  SCCS title bar.
-version:         Allows the user to specify a version to be used in 
		  the SCCS title bar.
-realschematics:  Generates .sue files that contain ICON procs, and SCHEMATIC
                  procs which consist of the actual circuits.

-nonhierdemorgan  Doesn't add the additional hierarchy for demorgans but
		  duplicates the schematic instead. Requires -realschematics.



Some Notes on the MMI Icons
===========================
The generic MMI icons are generators.  The options on the generators are Size 
(choices are taken from the mapfile) and DeMorgan.  The MMI icons have the 
following characteristics:

- If there is more than one input, the inputs are always named "in0", "in1",
  "in2", etc.  If there is only one input, its name is "in".
- In0 always connects to the fast node.
- The fast node is always indicated on the icon by the marker "F".
- The default rotation of the icon always draws the fast node at the top
  of the body, with the data flowing from left to right.
- Most icons have the same x and y dimensions to allow for easy substitution.
- DeMorganned symbols always have the same origin and pin locations as their
  non-DeMorganned counterparts.


Mapfile Examples
================
The general format for a single mapping is

{MMI_iconname
{Size Description 0}
{Size Description 1}
...
{Size Description n}
}

Size Description:
{Size {Non-DeMorganInfo} {DeMorgan-Info}}

Non-DeMorganInfo:
{schematicname {MMI_icon_pin0 schematic_pin0} {MMI_icon_pin1 schematic_pin1} ...} 

DeMorganInfo:
{schematicname {MMI_icon_pin0 schematic_pin0} {MMI_icon_pin1 schematic_pin1} ...} 


Line breaks and {} placements are important; follow the example exactly.  There must
be a line break after MMI_iconname; a line break after each Size description; no line
breaks within a Size description; and a final } on a line by itself.


Some notes:
- The user's schematic names can be arbitrary.
- Size descriptions can be in arbitrary order.  Note that the icon's default
  size is taken from the first Size description.
- Size can be indicated by any means the user likes, such as 0-4 or B-F.  The
  sizes need not be contiguous.
- If there is no DeMorgan version of the icon desired, omit the {DeMorgan-Info}
  from the Size Description.


Example 1
---------
The following is an example of a mode (1) mapfile, in which the user desires
icon-only .sue files.

---
# Map file for icon-only

{MMI_INV 
{A {MMI_INVA {in in} {out out}} {MMI_INVA_ {in in} {out out}}}
{B {MMI_INVB {in in} {out out}} {MMI_INVB_ {in in} {out out}}}
{C {MMI_INVC {in in} {out out}} {MMI_INVC_ {in in} {out out}}}
{D {MMI_INVD {in in} {out out}} {MMI_INVD_ {in in} {out out}}}
{E {MMI_INVE {in in} {out out}} {MMI_INVE_ {in in} {out out}}}
}

{MMI_AND2
{B {MMI_AND2B {in0 in0} {in1 in1} {out out}} {MMI_AND2B_ {in0 in0} {in1 in1} {out out}}}
{C {MMI_AND2C {in0 in0} {in1 in1} {out out}} {MMI_AND2C_ {in0 in0} {in1 in1} {out out}}}
}
---

This mapfile describes the mapping between the MMI_INV icon and 5 different
schematic sizes (A-E), as well as the mapping between MMI_AND2 and 2 schematic
sizes (B-C).  

Note that the user's "schematic" files do not actually need to exist; they
will not be used.  The pin "mapping" is also irrelevant, but needs to be
included in order to allow the mapper script to know the icon's pin names.
The icon will be generated with the size choices as described in the mapfile,
and will allow DeMorganning if the user has specified {DeMorgan-Info} in
the mapfile.


Example 2
---------
The following is an example of a mode (2) mapfile, in which the user describes
the mapping between the MMI icons and a pre-existing library:

---
# Map file for icons w/ user's library

{MMI_INV 
{A {INV0 {in in} {out out}} {INV0B {in in} {out out}}}
{B {INV1 {in in} {out out}} {INV1B {in in} {out out}}}
{C {INV2 {in in} {out out}} {INV2B {in in} {out out}}}
{D {INV3 {in in} {out out}} {INV3B {in in} {out out}}}
{H {INV7 {in in} {out out}} {INV7B {in in} {out out}}}
}

{MMI_AND2
{B {AND2B {in0 fred} {in1 ginger} {out swing}}}
{C {AND2C {in0 mary} {in1 johnny} {out hop}}}
}
---

This example illustrates the following features:
- The MMI icon and user icon pin names may be different.
- The user icon pin names need not be the same for all sizes,
  or even for DeMorgan vs. non-DeMorgan within a single size.
- The user's icon names may be unrelated to the MMI icon
  names; in this example, the user's icons are named INV0,
  INV0B, etc.
- The user must have separate icons for their own DeMorgan
  and non-DeMorganned versions.
- In this example, no DeMorganned version of MMI_AND2 will
  be created since no {DeMorgan-Info} is included for MMI_AND2.


Example 3
---------
The following is an example of a mode (3) mapfile, in which the user has drawn
schematic .sue files, and wishes to connect them to the MMI icons:

---
# Map file between MMI generic symbols and TSMC25 library

{MMI_INV 
{A {MMI_INVA {in in} {out out}} {MMI_INVA_ {in in} {out out}}}
{B {MMI_INVB {in in} {out out}} {MMI_INVB_ {in in} {out out}}}
{C {MMI_INVC {in in} {out out}} {MMI_INVC_ {in in} {out out}}}
{D {MMI_INVD {in in} {out out}} {MMI_INVD_ {in in} {out out}}}
{E {MMI_INVE {in in} {out out}} {MMI_INVE_ {in in} {out out}}}
}

{MMI_AND2
{0 {AND2B {in0 in0} {in1 in1} {out out}} {AND2BB {in0 in0} {in1 in1} {out out}}}
{1 {AND2C {in0 in0} {in1 in1} {out out}} {AND2CB {in0 in0} {in1 in1} {out out}}}
}
---

Some notes:
- In this mode, the icon_pin and schematic_pin names must match exactly.  
- The user's schematic names can be arbitrary.
- The user should NOT draw demorgan schematics; the {DeMorgan-Info} portion
  of a map description is not used in this mode (but must be present in
  the mapfile in order to indicate to the mapper that a demorganned version
  is desired.)


